<PolicyPage title="Editorial Policy - Love Mallacoota" description="What Love Mallacoota publishes, what it will not, and how advocacy, advertising and corrections are handled." canonicalPath="/editorial-policy.html" prefix="Editorial" accent="Policy" lede="Local information, open to everyone, corrected when it is wrong. Argue for the town; be kind about it.">
  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>Events, notices, group updates, directory listings, school and sport information, local history, family notices, classifieds and practical visitor information, supplied by the people or organisations it belongs to.</p>

  <h2>Arguing for the town is welcome</h2>
  <p>You may advocate here. A pool, better roads, a footpath, mobile coverage, how the shire spends its money, what should happen to a piece of land - these are the arguments a town has about its own future, and a local paper that refuses to carry them is not much use.</p>
  <p>That includes positions some people will disagree with, and it includes criticism of decisions made by councils, agencies and organisations. Disagreement is not incivility.</p>
  <p><strong>The rule is simple: be kind.</strong> Argue with the decision, not the person who made it. Someone reading your piece should be able to disagree with you and still sit next to you at the golf club.</p>

  <h2>What we do not publish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Personal attacks, or anything that reads as a pile-on against a named individual.</li>
    <li>Unverified allegations about named people or businesses. Bring the evidence, or write about the issue rather than the person.</li>
    <li>Discriminatory material.</li>
    <li>Anything that places someone at unreasonable risk, including a person's home address or movements without their agreement.</li>
    <li>Anything presenting itself as an emergency authority.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Politics and candidates</h2>
  <p>We publish political articles, on one condition: they must be truthful. Argue for what you believe, criticise a decision or a policy, make your case - but do not assert as fact something you cannot stand behind. We will correct anything that turns out to be wrong, visibly, whoever wrote it.</p>
  <p>At election time we will approach candidates standing in seats covering Mallacoota and ask each of them the same question: <em>what will you do for this town?</em> Their answers run as they write them, side by side, with equal space. If a candidate does not reply, we will say so rather than leave a gap.</p>
  <p>This is not the same as endorsement. We do not endorse candidates, and we will not run a piece that reads as a personal attack on one.</p>

  <h2>Advertising, including political advertising</h2>
  <p>We carry advertising, and we accept political advertising. It is always labelled as an advertisement and never dressed as editorial.</p>
  <p>Electoral advertising in Australia must carry an authorisation - the name and address of the person or organisation who authorised it. We will not publish an electoral advertisement without one, and it appears with the advertisement rather than buried.</p>
  <p>Accepting an advertisement is not an endorsement, and refusing one is not a judgement on the cause. We may decline anything that would breach the rest of this policy.</p>

  <h2>Sources and attribution</h2>
  <p>We identify the source organisation or person where useful, link to authoritative material, credit photographs, and distinguish sponsored material from editorial or community information.</p>

  <h2>Who decides, and how</h2>
  <p>Contributions from approved contributors publish straight through, after an automated check against this policy. Anything the check queries is held for a person to read, and the contributor is told which part of the policy it queried and why. Held is not rejected.</p>
  <p>Colin Dixon is the publisher and is responsible for everything on this site, including anything the automated check passed.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>We correct things visibly rather than quietly. A correction is recorded on the article, with its date, so the record shows what changed. See <a href="/corrections.html">corrections</a>.</p>

  <h2>Emergency information</h2>
  <p>We relay official information only and never present Love Mallacoota as an emergency authority. See the <a href="/emergency.html">emergency information page</a>.</p>
</PolicyPage>
